        <div class="legend"><span><b>Underlined row</b> primary key</span><span><b>PK, FK</b> both key types</span><span><b>Arrow direction</b> FK to referenced PK</span><span><b>UQ</b> unique constraint</span><span><b>virtual</b> Oracle generated column, not stored</span></div>
      </div></div>
      <div class="mapping"><b>Mapping notes:</b> Name and Address are flattened into their parts. Multivalued PhoneNo becomes USER_PHONE. Derived attributes become Oracle virtual columns (AvailableQuantity, TotalAmount, StorageFee, MinimumReleaseDate); Age is left to the query layer. The specialization becomes parent plus five subclass tables whose primary key is also a foreign key to USERS. Both weak entities take the owner key plus their partial key. Both ternaries become tables carrying three foreign key sets plus their own relationship attributes, with a UNIQUE across the three so a triple cannot repeat. The first aggregation is realised by SALE_ORDER referencing BID with a UNIQUE, which is what makes it one order per winning bid. The second is realised by STORES carrying its own surrogate key AllocationID, which STORAGE_PAYMENT then references. Oracle 11g has no IDENTITY column, so every surrogate key is a sequence plus a BEFORE INSERT trigger.</div>
    </section>

    <section id="task-c">
      <h2>Task C - Assumptions</h2>
      <ol class="assumptions"><li>Every user is exactly one of farmer, buyer, admin staff, storage manager, or transport personnel — the specialization is total and disjoint, so a person who both farms and buys needs two accounts.</li><li>UserID is unique across all five subclasses, and each subclass table reuses it as both primary key and foreign key rather than issuing its own.</li><li>A phone number belongs to exactly one user, so USER_PHONE carries a UNIQUE on PhoneNo as well as the composite primary key.</li><li>There is no AUCTION entity. One bidding window per batch made it 1:1 with HARVEST_BATCH, so MinimumPrice, BiddingStartTime and BiddingEndTime live on the batch itself.</li><li>A bid must be at least the batch MinimumPrice and strictly greater than the current highest bid; MinimumPrice must itself be at least the crop BasePrice.</li><li>A buyer may hold only one ACTIVE bid per batch. Placing a higher one moves the previous highest to OUTBID and records it in PreviousBidID.</li><li>Exactly one sale order arises from one winning bid, enforced by UNIQUE on SALE_ORDER.BidID — this is the aggregation made concrete.</li><li>Payment is direct from buyer to farmer. There is no ARAT commission and no escrow, so PAYMENT references buyer, farmer and sale order together.</li><li>Payment on delivery terms is allowed only once the transport request reads DELIVERED; total payments may not exceed the order total.</li><li>Storage has two legs: a farmer storing an unsold batch, and a buyer storing a purchased order. Exactly one of RequestedByFarmerID or RequestedByBuyerID is set on any allocation.</li><li>A storage manager proposes an allocation and the customer accepts it; nothing is in storage until then. Releasing before the minimum term needs both parties to agree.</li><li>Unit numbers restart at 1 in every warehouse, so STORAGE_UNIT is weak and identified by (WarehouseID, UnitNo).</li><li>A bazar daily record is identified by (BazarID, RecordDate) — one record per bazar per day — and is deleted with its bazar.</li><li>A daily market price is unique per (CropID, AratID, PriceDate); the admin who logged it is recorded but optional.</li><li>An arat may have at most one parent arat, and the hierarchy is acyclic, so CONNECT BY NOCYCLE terminates.</li><li>One review per completed sale order, enforced by UNIQUE on REVIEW.SaleOrderID — a review is written against the ORDER, not against a payment. Complaints are unlimited per order.</li><li>STORES is aggregated: once the ternary has its own key an allocation becomes referenceable as a single fact, which is what STORAGE_PAYMENT points at. A storage fee may be paid in instalments, so settled by is 1:N and total on the payment side.</li><li>Every user may receive notifications; NOTIFICATION references USERS directly rather than any subclass, since all five roles are notified the same way.</li><li>AvailableQuantity, TotalAmount, StorageFee and MinimumReleaseDate are Oracle virtual columns — derived, never stored independently. Age is derived in the query layer and not stored at all.</li><li>A vehicle must have capacity at least the load it carries, and one trip pairs one request with one vehicle and one driver.</li></ol>
    </section>

    <section id="guide">
      <h2>How to Convert a Scenario into an E-R Diagram and Schema</h2>
      <p class="intro">Use this before drawing. Model the real-world meaning first, then convert the finished E-R diagram into relations.</p>
      <div class="guide-grid"><article class="guide-card"><h3>1. Find the entities</h3><ul><li>An entity is an independent noun the system must remember facts about: farmer, crop, batch, warehouse, vehicle.</li><li>A strong entity has a key of its own. A weak entity does not — a storage unit means nothing without its warehouse.</li><li>Resist promoting every noun. District describes a farm; it is an attribute, not an entity.</li><li>Delete an entity that adds no information. AUCTION was 1:1 with HARVEST_BATCH, so it became three columns on the batch.</li></ul></article><article class="guide-card"><h3>2. Classify the attributes</h3><ul><li>Simple: one stored value, like BasePrice.</li><li>Composite: split into parts you will query separately — Name into first/middle/last, Address into road/district/postcode.</li><li>Multivalued: many values per entity, like PhoneNo. Double ellipse, and its own table when mapped.</li><li>Derived: computable from what is already stored, like AvailableQuantity. Dashed ellipse, and an Oracle virtual column when mapped.</li></ul></article><article class="guide-card"><h3>3. Set cardinality and participation</h3><ul><li>Write 1, N, M or P on every edge, on the side it applies to.</li><li>A double line means total participation: every bid must attach to a batch, so BID is total on that edge.</li><li>Put an attribute on the relationship when the fact belongs to the event rather than either entity.</li><li>QuantityStored belongs to a particular allocation, not to the batch and not to the unit.</li></ul></article><article class="guide-card"><h3>4. Spot the ternary</h3><ul><li>Use a ternary when one fact needs three participants at the same moment.</li><li>Test it by removal: drop one leg and see whether the fact survives.</li><li>Splitting STORES into batch-unit and manager-unit loses which manager authorised that allocation, which is the accountability record in a spoilage dispute.</li><li>Splitting ASSIGNED_TO loses which driver took which vehicle on which trip.</li></ul><div class="mini-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 1280 500" aria-label="Ternary relationships">
<text class="label bold" x="620" y="26" style="font-size:17px;fill:#1d4b2c">Two ternary relationships</text>
<rect class="entity" x="30" y="90" width="200" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="130.0" y="119.0" style="font-size:13px">HARVEST_BATCH</text>
<rect class="entity" x="30" y="300" width="200" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="130.0" y="329.0" style="font-size:13px">STORAGE_UNIT</text>
<rect class="entity" x="330" y="400" width="220" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="440.0" y="429.0" style="font-size:12px">STORAGE_MANAGER</text>
<polygon class="relationship" points="240,224 330,174 420,224 330,274"/>
<text class="label bold" x="330" y="224" style="font-size:14px">STORES</text>
<path class="connector" d="M230 119 L262 190"/>
<text class="label bold" x="232" y="160" style="font-size:16px">M</text>
<path class="connector" d="M230 329 L266 258"/>
<text class="label bold" x="232" y="300" style="font-size:16px">N</text>
<path class="connector" d="M370 274 L420 400"/>
<text class="label bold" x="410" y="348" style="font-size:16px">P</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="200" cy="60" rx="64" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="200" y="60" style="font-size:12px">QuantityStored</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="470" cy="96" rx="40" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="470" y="96" style="font-size:12px">DateIn</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="540" cy="170" rx="40" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="540" y="170" style="font-size:12px">DateOut</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="560" cy="250" rx="71" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="560" y="250" style="font-size:12px">AllocationStatus</text>
<path class="connector thin" d="M240 82 L300 178"/>
<path class="connector thin" d="M432 112 L372 186"/>
<path class="connector thin" d="M501 174 L404 206"/>
<path class="connector thin" d="M493 248 L416 234"/>
<text x="300" y="480" style="font-size:12px;text-anchor:middle;dominant-baseline:middle;fill:#4a5c6d">Drops which manager authorised the allocation if split into binaries.</text>
<rect class="entity" x="700" y="90" width="230" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="815.0" y="119.0" style="font-size:12px">TRANSPORT_REQUEST</text>
<rect class="entity" x="700" y="300" width="200" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="800.0" y="329.0" style="font-size:13px">VEHICLE</text>
<rect class="entity" x="990" y="400" width="240" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="1110.0" y="429.0" style="font-size:11px">TRANSPORT_PERSONNEL</text>
<polygon class="relationship" points="900,224 1000,174 1100,224 1000,274"/>
<text class="label bold" x="1000" y="224" style="font-size:13px">ASSIGNED_TO</text>
<path class="connector" d="M930 119 L905 190"/>
<text class="label bold" x="902" y="160" style="font-size:16px">M</text>
<path class="connector" d="M900 329 L920 262"/>
<text class="label bold" x="902" y="300" style="font-size:16px">N</text>
<path class="connector" d="M1050 270 L1090 400"/>
<text class="label bold" x="1080" y="348" style="font-size:16px">P</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="1150" cy="120" rx="57" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="1150" y="120" style="font-size:12px">AssignedDate</text>
<ellipse class="attribute" cx="1180" cy="200" rx="71" ry="24"/>
<text class="label" x="1180" y="200" style="font-size:12px">AssignmentStatus</text>
<path class="connector thin" d="M1093 132 L1048 184"/>
<path class="connector thin" d="M1109 206 L1098 220"/>
<text x="960" y="480" style="font-size:12px;text-anchor:middle;dominant-baseline:middle;fill:#4a5c6d">One trip = one request + one vehicle + one driver, decided together.</text>
</svg></div></article><article class="guide-card"><h3>5. Spot the aggregation</h3><ul><li>Use aggregation when a relationship occurrence itself participates in another relationship.</li><li>Ask what the outer entity really refers to. A sale order does not refer to a buyer, a bid, or a batch on their own.</li><li>It refers to the combined fact that this buyer's bid on this batch won.</li><li>Relating SALE_ORDER separately to all three would allow a row whose buyer, bid and batch disagree with each other.</li></ul><div class="mini-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 1240 492" aria-label="Aggregation">
<text class="label bold" x="620" y="26" style="font-size:17px;fill:#1d4b2c">Aggregation</text>
<rect x="40" y="60" width="700" height="345" fill="none" stroke="#2f7d52" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-dasharray="12 8"/>
<text class="label bold small" x="390" y="86" style="fill:#2f7d52;font-size:13px">the fact that this buyer's bid on this batch won</text>
<rect class="entity" x="80" y="130" width="190" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="175.0" y="159.0" style="font-size:15px">BUYER</text>
<polygon class="relationship" points="314,159 370,129 426,159 370,189"/>
<text class="label bold" x="370" y="159" style="font-size:12px">places</text>
<rect class="entity" x="510" y="130" width="190" height="58"/>
<text class="label bold" x="605.0" y="159.0" style="font-size:15px">BID</text>
<path class="connector" d="M270 159 H314"/>
<text class="label bold" x="288" y="142" style="font-size:16px">1</text>
<path class="connector" d="M426 162 L510 162"/><path class="connector" d="M426 156 L510 156"/>
<text class="label bold" x="468" y="142" style="font-size:16px">N</text>
<polygon class="relationship" points="557,246 605,218 653,246 605,274"/>
<text class="label bold" x="605" y="246" style="font-size:12px">on</text>
<path class="connector" d="M602 188 L602 218"/><path class="connector" d="M608 188 L608 218"/>
<text class="label bold" x="628" y="204" style="font-size:16px">N</text>
<rect class="entity" x="490" y="302" width="230" height="62"/>
<text class="label bold" x="605.0" y="333.0" style="font-size:14px">HARVEST_BATCH</text>
<path class="connector" d="M605 274 V302"/>
<text class="label bold" x="628" y="290" style="font-size:16px">1</text>
<polygon class="relationship" points="778,210 840,178 902,210 840,242"/>
<text class="label bold" x="840" y="210" style="font-size:12px">creates</text>
<path class="connector" d="M740 210 H778"/>
<text class="label bold" x="756" y="192" style="font-size:16px">1</text>
<rect class="entity" x="990" y="180" width="220" height="62"/>
<text class="label bold" x="1100.0" y="211.0" style="font-size:15px">SALE_ORDER</text>
<path class="connector" d="M902 212 L990 212"/><path class="connector" d="M902 208 L990 208"/>
<text class="label bold" x="946" y="192" style="font-size:16px">1</text>
<text x="620" y="442" style="font-size:12px;text-anchor:middle;dominant-baseline:middle;fill:#4a5c6d">The line leaves the dashed box, not the BID rectangle: SALE_ORDER relates to the whole buyer-bid-batch fact.</text>
<text x="620" y="466" style="font-size:12px;text-anchor:middle;dominant-baseline:middle;fill:#4a5c6d">UQ(SALE_ORDER.BidID) enforces the 1:1.</text>
</svg></div></article><article class="guide-card"><h3>6. Map it to relations</h3><ul><li>Strong entity becomes a table with its key and simple attributes.</li><li>Composite attribute stores only its parts, never the parent.</li><li>Multivalued attribute becomes its own table: owner key plus value as the composite primary key.</li><li>Derived attribute becomes a virtual column or is left to the query.</li><li>Weak entity takes owner key plus partial key as its primary key, with the owner key also a foreign key and ON DELETE CASCADE.</li><li>1:N puts the foreign key on the N side; M:N becomes its own table.</li><li>Ternary becomes one table holding all three foreign key sets plus the relationship attributes.</li><li>Specialization becomes parent plus subclass tables, each subclass primary key also a foreign key upward.</li><li>Aggregation maps the inner relationship first, then the outer table references its key.</li></ul></article></div>
      <div class="mapping"><b>Exam shortcut:</b> underline every primary key, mark every foreign key, point every arrow at the key it references, and write an assumption wherever a date, an optional rule or a participation is not fully pinned down by the scenario.</div>
    </section>
